session:pr632Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #62 asked to port six named eval.rs functions (eval_node_kind, eval_datatype, eval_range, eval_regex_check, eval_language_in, eval_less_than) to Datalog FilterAtom rules per EXPRESSION_PLAN.md Phase E4. Orientation found the plan doc stale on two fronts: those six Rust functions no longer exist under those names (the logic now lives in shared helpers), and a second consumer of that logic — constraint_conforms, the recursive sh:and/or/not/xone/node/qualifiedValueShape conformance checker — did not exist when the plan was written, so porting the top-level producer alone does not let the helpers be deleted. Before writing any rule I traced each of the six constraint kinds against sparql_parser's actual expression evaluator (execute/expressions.rs, execute/functions.rs) rather than assuming the plan's example FILTER expressions would work. sh:nodeKind and sh:languageIn checked out cleanly: ISIRI/ISBLANK/ISLITERAL match GraphElement variants directly with no lossy conversion, and LANG()/LANGMATCHES() correctly fall through to 'does not match' for non-language-tagged or non-literal values with no special-casing, matching the exact semantics fixed by earlier issues #266/#303. The other four did not: compare_graph_elements (the generic SPARQL </>/<=/>= evaluator) is weaker than SHACL's own sparql_compare and falls back to naive string comparison across mismatched datatypes instead of flagging them incomparable, which is precisely the bug class #303/#322/#325 fixed for the Rust path; REGEX's text argument goes through graph_element_to_string, which drops natively-typed numeric/date/boolean literals that lexical_form (the Rust path) handles; and sh:lessThan/sh:lessThanOrEquals additionally need one violation fact per failing (value, otherValue) pair via runtime-minted discriminated predicates (#343), which a single Datalog rule head cannot produce. Implemented only sh:nodeKind and sh:languageIn: added prop_constraint_rules arms in translate.rs building FilterAtom-guarded rules for both the property-shape-scoped (with sh:path) and node-shape-scoped (pathless, via the dedicated ParsedShape::node_kind field) cases, removed the now-redundant eval_prop_constraint arms and the node_kind block in eval_all from evaluate.rs, but left matches_node_kind/lang_matches themselves in place since constraint_conforms still calls them. Updated EXPRESSION_PLAN.md Phase E4 with a corrected, per-constraint verdict table so the next agent doesn't re-derive this from scratch, and filed #631 for the four deferred constraints with the specific sparql_parser gaps that block them. All 175 shacl_suite.rs tests and 10 w3c_shacl_suite.rs tests (no skip-list changes) plus full CI (fmt/clippy/tests/release-tests/docs/audit) passed unchanged." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:632 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr632Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr632 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr632Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Ported only sh:nodeKind and sh:languageIn to FilterAtom Datalog rules; left sh:datatype, the four range constraints, sh:pattern, and sh:lessThan/sh:lessThanOrEquals as hand-coded Rust in evaluate.rs, filing #631 to track them once sparql_parser's comparator/string-coercion functions are brought up to full SPARQL 1.1 fidelity." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered porting all six constraint kinds named in the issue text directly, reusing the plan doc's example FILTER expressions as-is (e.g. FILTER(v < minVal) for sh:range, FILTER(!REGEX(STR(v), pat)) for sh:pattern). Rejected after checking sparql_parser's actual evaluator: its generic comparison and string-coercion functions are measurably weaker than SHACL's own comparator/lexical_form helpers (verified by reading compare_graph_elements and graph_element_to_string directly, not just by running tests), so a naive port would have silently regressed exactly the W3C SHACL suite bugs #303/#322/#325/#343 previously fixed in Rust. Shipping fewer, verified-correct constraints was judged better than shipping more constraints with an unverified risk of silent regressions the existing test suite might not even catch (the suite is a finite fixture set, not an exhaustive comparator conformance check)." .
